* Raw RSA oracle forgery (ePrint 2026/2131) - Splunk.
*
* Companion to detections/sigma/rsa-raw-oracle.yml and detections/kql/rsa-raw-oracle-hunting.kql.
*
* SCHEMA. Uses the neutral field names from the pack README: key_label, operation, mechanism,
* client_id. Index and sourcetype names below are placeholders:
*   index=hsm        sourcetype=hsm:crypto        one event per private-key operation
*   index=verify     sourcetype=signature:seen    one event per signature observed verifying
*   index=tokens     sourcetype=token:issue / token:redeem
*   lookup signing_ledger.csv                     every signature your key produced
* Rename to your own. The logic survives the translation, the names do not.
*
* ORDER. Search 1 is the only one that still works after the attacker has left, and the only one
* blinding cannot defeat. Deploy it first even though it is the hardest to feed.
*
* Ref: https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2131
* Author: Cristian Ruvalcaba and the Saluca Agentic AI Research Team, 2026-09-26
* Apache-2.0. Defensive use only.


* ============================================================================================
* SEARCH 1 - Signing-ledger reconciliation. ALERT.
*
* A forged signature verifies. It is indistinguishable from a real one at the verifier. The ONLY
* thing that can tell them apart is your own record of what you signed. Anything that verifies
* under your key and is not in the ledger was not produced by your signer.
*
* Feed: signatures you observe in use (CT logs for certificates, code-signing verification
* telemetry, API gateways that verify signed requests), hashed as sha256 of the signature bytes.
* Ledger: the same hash, written by the signing service at the moment it signs. Write the ledger
* from the signing path itself, not from a later export, or a gap in the export reads as forgery.
* ============================================================================================

index=verify sourcetype=signature:seen verified=true
| stats min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen, count AS times_seen,
        values(seen_at) AS seen_at
        BY key_label, sig_sha256
| lookup signing_ledger.csv sig_sha256 OUTPUT key_label AS ledger_key, signed_time
| where isnull(ledger_key)
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| table key_label, sig_sha256, first_seen, last_seen, times_seen, seen_at


* ============================================================================================
* SEARCH 2 - Cumulative private-key operation budget per key. ALERT once budgets are set.
*
* The attack needs ~2^32 raw queries at 1024 bits, ~2^43 at 2048 (the authors' extrapolation).
* Rate-based rules miss a patient attacker, so this is a RUNNING TOTAL per key since the key was
* created or last rotated, compared to a forecast.
*
* key_budgets.csv columns: key_label, key_bits, created_time (epoch), budget_ops, owner, reason.
* Set budget_ops from observed legitimate volume with headroom, and document it. A key with no
* budget row is reported, not ignored: unknown is a finding, not a pass.
*
* Run over the key's whole lifetime. For high-volume keys, feed this from a summary index
* populated daily rather than scanning raw events.
* ============================================================================================

index=hsm sourcetype=hsm:crypto operation IN ("sign","decrypt")
| stats count AS ops_total,
        sum(eval(if(in(mechanism,"CKM_RSA_X_509","RSA-X-509","RSA_NO_PADDING"),1,0))) AS raw_ops,
        dc(client_id) AS distinct_clients,
        min(_time) AS first_op, max(_time) AS last_op
        BY key_label
| lookup key_budgets.csv key_label OUTPUT budget_ops, key_bits, owner
| eval status=case(isnull(budget_ops), "NO BUDGET - unknown is a finding",
                   ops_total >= budget_ops, "OVER BUDGET",
                   ops_total >= 0.8 * budget_ops, "80% OF BUDGET - rotate",
                   true(), "ok")
| where status!="ok"
| eval attack_scale=case(key_bits<=1024 AND raw_ops>=pow(2,30), "raw ops within 4x of the 1024-bit attack (2^32)",
                         key_bits<=2048 AND raw_ops>=pow(2,40), "raw ops within 8x of the 2048-bit estimate (2^43)",
                         true(), "")
| convert ctime(first_op) ctime(last_op)
| table key_label, key_bits, owner, ops_total, raw_ops, distinct_clients, budget_ops, status, attack_scale, first_op, last_op


* ============================================================================================
* SEARCH 3 - Blind RSA token issuers: redemptions must never exceed issuances. ALERT.
*
* For RFC 9474 deployments (Privacy Pass publicly verifiable tokens and similar) there is NO
* query-content detection: the attacker's blinded queries are indistinguishable from real ones.
* What a forger cannot hide is that they redeem tokens nobody issued. Per key, the count of
* distinct redeemed tokens can never legitimately exceed the count issued under that key.
*
* Also shows cumulative issuance per key, which is the budget signal for issuers: rotate each key
* long before it approaches your planning threshold, because the precomputation depends on the
* public key and rotation throws it away.
* ============================================================================================

(index=tokens sourcetype=token:issue) OR (index=tokens sourcetype=token:redeem)
| eval is_issue=if(sourcetype="token:issue",1,0), is_redeem=if(sourcetype="token:redeem",1,0)
| stats sum(is_issue) AS issued,
        dc(eval(if(is_redeem=1, token_id, null()))) AS redeemed_distinct,
        min(_time) AS first_event, max(_time) AS last_event
        BY key_label
| eval surplus=redeemed_distinct - issued
| where surplus > 0 OR issued >= 1000000000
| eval finding=if(surplus > 0, "REDEMPTIONS EXCEED ISSUANCE - forgery or a broken issuance log",
                               "high cumulative issuance - check rotation schedule")
| convert ctime(first_event) ctime(last_event)
| table key_label, issued, redeemed_distinct, surplus, finding, first_event, last_event


* ============================================================================================
* SEARCH 4 - Input-shape tripwire. HUNT.
*
* Only for signers that log the raw input (for example a gateway in front of an HSM that pads in
* software). As published, the attack queries small integers: every prime up to 2^36, and values
* a - b*m far below the modulus. A legitimately padded input is full width. So an input whose bit
* length is far below the modulus size is either a bug or this attack.
*
* WHY THIS IS ONLY A HUNT. Anyone with the public key can blind their queries (multiply by r^e)
* so they look uniformly random, and unblind the answers afterwards. This catches the attack as
* the researchers ran it, and a careless copy of it. It does not catch a careful attacker, and a
* quiet result is not evidence of safety. Use the padding-enforcement gate described in the pack
* README as the control; it is not defeated by blinding.
*
* raw_input_bits: bit length of the integer submitted to the raw operation, computed by the
* gateway at log time (logging the value itself is unnecessary and may be sensitive).
* ============================================================================================

index=hsm sourcetype=hsm:crypto mechanism IN ("CKM_RSA_X_509","RSA-X-509","RSA_NO_PADDING") raw_input_bits=*
| lookup key_budgets.csv key_label OUTPUT key_bits
| eval short_by=key_bits - raw_input_bits
| where short_by >= 64
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS short_inputs, min(raw_input_bits) AS smallest_bits, dc(client_id) AS clients,
        values(client_id) AS client_ids
        BY _time, key_label, key_bits
| where short_inputs >= 10
| sort - short_inputs
